Inguinal Hernia
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Visible reducible groin bulge, Increase with Valsalva, Dull dragging groin pain

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-039
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of sharp, severe pain localized to a known groin bulge, accompanied by an inability to push the lump back in and new-onset nausea.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient only.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a sudden change in the character of a previously reducible groin hernia, marked by acute, severe localized pain, irreducibility, and nausea. This presentation is a hallmark for an acutely incarcerated or impending strangulated inguinal hernia. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly mandated to evaluate for bowel compromise, attempt manual reduction under analgesia, and arrange immediate surgical referral if reduction fails prior to the onset of ischemic necrosis.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The lump in my groin has always popped back in when I lie down, but today it is stuck, rock hard, and hurts so badly I feel sick."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 6-month history of a visible bulge in the right groin, noting a dull aching, dragging, and heavy sensation that worsens after long shifts of physical labor. Historically, the bulge size increased with standing, coughing, or Valsalva manoeuvres, and showed complete reduction or disappearance when lying supine. Today, after lifting a heavy box 3 hours ago, the patient experienced sudden, sharp, localized pain in the groin. The mass is now exquisitely tender and irreducible. Reports new-onset severe nausea and one episode of non-bilious vomiting.
Functional Status: Lying supine with knees bent to relieve tension on the groin, unable to stand or walk due to severe pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of bowel strangulation and necrosis.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 (Pain mediated) | HR: 105 | Temp: 37.2 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Patient is diaphoretic, anxious, and in severe localized pain.
Physical Exam: Visual inspection reveals a tense, distinct, 5 cm swelling in the right inguinal region extending toward the scrotum. The overlying skin is slightly erythematous. Gentle palpation confirms a firm, exquisitely tender mass protruding through the superficial inguinal ring. The mass is completely irreducible despite patient positioning (supine with knees flexed). Bowel sounds are absent over the mass. Abdomen is slightly distended with mild generalized discomfort. The underlying pathology explicitly demonstrates a previously visible reducible groin bulge, increase with Valsalva, and dull dragging groin pain, which has now acutely transitioned to an incarcerated state.
Environmental Observations: Patient is lying on the floor.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Incarcerated Inguinal Hernia - Status: Exacerbated (Impending strangulation)
Differentials:

Testicular Torsion - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pathology being localized primarily to the inguinal canal rather than the testicle itself, the preceding 6-month history of a reducible groin mass, and the presence of the mass extending from the external ring.

Inguinal Lymphadenitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The hyperacute onset related to heavy lifting, the immense size and tension of the mass, and the lack of a primary lower extremity infectious source.

Hydrocele - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute, severe pain, the firm/hard nature of the swelling (rather than fluctuant/cystic), and the presence of nausea and vomiting indicating bowel involvement.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical risk is critically high)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acutely incarcerated inguinal hernia, rapidly progressing toward strangulation (ischemia of the entrapped bowel). The objective findings of an irreducible, tense, exquisitely tender mass accompanied by nausea and tachycardia mandate immediate escalation of care. Urgent out-of-hours evaluation was absolutely necessary to definitively diagnose the incarceration and attempt gentle manual reduction using Trendelenburg positioning and analgesia. As the hernia remained firmly irreducible and acutely tender during the visit, immediate emergency transport for surgical intervention was required to salvage the entrapped bowel before irreversible gangrene and perforation occurred overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ketorolac 30mg and IM Ondansetron 4mg. Placed patient in the Trendelenburg position (head down, pelvis elevated) and applied an ice pack to the groin for 15 minutes to reduce localized oedema. Attempted a single, gentle trial of manual reduction which was unsuccessful. Arranged immediate emergency ambulance transport for urgent surgical evaluation.
Positive Behavioral Change: Remain completely flat with a pillow under your buttocks to elevate your pelvis while waiting for the ambulance. This specific angle uses gravity to pull the heavy intra-abdominal bowel away from the groin defect, immediately reducing the crushing pressure on the trapped segment and giving it the best chance of surviving until surgery.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute surgical admission required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance is en route. If the pain suddenly disappears completely but the lump turns dark purple or black, or if you begin vomiting dark, foul-smelling material, alert the paramedics immediately upon arrival as this indicates the bowel has died and burst.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a surgical emergency requiring immediate hospital intervention to release the trapped tissue.
Clinical Course: The hospital team will likely need to perform emergency surgery tonight to open the muscle ring, push the bowel back inside, and repair the defect with a mesh to prevent recurrence.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to forcefully push or squeeze the lump back in yourself. The bowel wall is currently severely swollen and fragile; aggressive force will cause it to rupture, releasing faeces into your abdomen and causing a life-threatening infection.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A weakness in your abdominal wall has allowed a loop of your bowel to push through into your groin. Because you lifted a heavy weight, a larger segment of bowel was forced through the tight muscle ring and is now physically trapped (incarcerated). The tight ring is acting like a tourniquet, cutting off the blood supply to that piece of bowel. The severe pain and nausea are the bowel's alarm signals that it is dying. Emergency surgery is required immediately to cut the constricting ring and restore blood flow before the tissue turns gangrenous.
Actionable Steps: Strictly do not eat or drink anything. The surgical team requires your stomach to be completely empty so they can safely administer a general anaesthetic the moment you arrive at the hospital.

